\f1\fs22 Sarah locked her office door and sat at her desk so fast, you\'92d think it was a sports car. She raced to warn Mr Fairy Boxers before the dark man with a particle pistol and ominous present-tense sentences found him. She knew she should care nothing for a Cloudborne, especially a manual labourer, but there was no shaking the feeling she may have set the dark man on his trail. 
\f2\i What is this feeling? Is it guilt? No
\f1\i0 , she decided. 
\f2\i It\'92s worse. It\'92s embarrassment.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stroked her holoscreen with the grace of a poet. Let others deride it and never suspect its capabilities. With a flick, she spun a dizzying forest of three-dimensional business documents around her. Beneath their canopy, she blinked a small console alive and produced a keyboard\'97yes, a keyboard!\'97and discreetly typed. Faster than the executive overviews around her could make an insomniac unconscious, she plunged deep inside office security, downloading surveillance logs and reconstituting half-overwritten image streams.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alas. Her glee, once as effervescent as champagne bubbles, fizzled into pursed-lipped disappointment. Nowhere in the scrambled data did she find the enigmatic dark man lurking in reception, marching awkwardly in the hallway, or slithering in Breezy Brains\'92 insufficiently sterile office. No particle pistol, no aura of menace.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah ran the logs through image processors looking for editing artefacts. Nothing. The cold emptiness of the dark man\'92s absence was now pregnant with her own dread. This kind of manipulation required stratospheric authority\'97likely a spook. A PAPER spook, the worst kind of intelligence agency.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax me into poverty,
\f1\i0  Sarah thought. 
\f2\i I sent this loon after Mr Fairy Boxers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah spent the morning gathering everything known about Tony Chantara of Enchanted Cleaning Services. 
\f2\i Everything
\f1\i0  amounted to very little, but the spaces where data had been were as obvious as tooth decay, some deleted as recently as that morning. All she uncovered: a birth certificate (father unknown), patchy school records (noting his obstreperousness despite poor grades), an Enchanted Cleaning Services business listing (no address), and drunk-and-disorderly fines. No consumer records, no marketing algorithms appraising him worthy of spamming. When she searched for malware attacks against him: none.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Was Mr Fairy Boxers too dull to surveil?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stared out the window. On another day, she might shrug off a creepy agent. But the cleaner couldn\'92t mend his own overalls\'97he\'92d be no match for a PAPER agent. She\'92d have to warn him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At 1:30pm, she took a hover-copt to attend a company-mandated appointment at a clinic for samples, examinations, and radiation bombardment. As expected, she passed with perfect health. One result gave her pause:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qc\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>PREGNANCY: negative
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah went home and resumed searching for Mr Fairy Boxers\'97an address, a watering hole, anything. Desperation drove her to gather a cloak, tie her hair back, and hover-copt to The Pale, doing rounds of karaoke bars where vanilla Cloudborne mixed with Elvi at the edge of The Ghetto. Chanta seemed the kind who might slum it with classes even lower than himself. The thought made her smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her dim hunch ignited into a solid lead at the Viva Vegas Club, where waiter Danny-Sue knew of Enchanted Cleaning, Chanta, and the tear in his overalls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Go check out the Stickett Inn, sugar,\'94 Danny-Sue told Sarah. \'93That\'92s where his bodyguards usually hole him up.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah found Tony at the Stickett Inn, flanked by three scruffs who looked like a pirate, a fence, and a contract killer. Bodyguards indeed. Not long after, the bomb exploded.
